hint: Left subtree (20, 30, 40), then 50, then the right subtree (60, 70, 80).
right: `[20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80] True`: when smaller values always sit on the left, inorder lists them in sorted order.
wrong: Inorder: 30's subtree gives 20, 30, 40; then 50; then 70's subtree gives 60, 70, 80. That list is already sorted.

=== task: predict hard
title: Prefix and postfix
code:
```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, val, left=None, right=None):
        self.val = val
        self.left = left
        self.right = right

def preorder(node, out):
    if node is None:
        return
    out.append(node.val)
    preorder(node.left, out)
    preorder(node.right, out)

def postorder(node, out):
    if node is None:
        return
    postorder(node.left, out)
    postorder(node.right, out)
    out.append(node.val)

expr = Node("-", Node("*", Node("a"), Node("b")), Node("c"))
pre, post = [], []
preorder(expr, pre)
postorder(expr, post)
print(" ".join(pre))
print(" ".join(post))
```
wrong[- * a b c\na b c * -]: Postorder finishes the left subtree, `a b *`, before it visits c.
wrong[* - a b c\na b * c -]: Preorder starts with the root, `-`.
wrong[- * a b c\nc b a * -]: Postorder visits the left subtree first: a, b, then *.
hint: The tree is (a * b) - c: `-` at the root, `*` on its left with a and b below, and c on its right.
hint: Preorder puts each operator before its parts; postorder puts it after.
right: `- * a b c` is prefix notation and `a b * c -` is postfix notation for (a * b) - c.
wrong: Preorder: -, then the left subtree *, a, b, then c. Postorder: a, b, *, then c, then -.

